# Chroma library, modern Earth: THE SPHERES OF SOCIETY, the levers, felt fairness and the forum. Library thread,
# 2026-10-09, "v22" update stage 4, item 15 phase 4 (chroma-ideas/spheres-implementation.md, "Phase 4: your mark on the
# town"): options drawn from the 81 lever lines (character.levers in each <sphere>.json; what each lever means at each
# rung, dynamics.json levers), felt fairness (Tyler 1990) and the forum.
# Every moment carries sphere:, every option its lever: and the face it acts in (sphere: gather.R; a cross option X>Y the
# means colour's face; a pair option the wheel-order pair face, WR as .RW, WG as .GW, UG as .GU).
# Rungs: moments opening from newcomer or regular offer exit, voice, loyalty, neglect, subvert and found; moments opening
# from known, pillar or leader add lead, office and fund. A rate on a moment with ladder: is the rate among people at
# that rung (the Engine gates the moment by the rung), and times: says so.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: SPEC.md. Words for faces and places: earth_spheres.py (no face named by colour).
# Evenness: inside each moment every one-colour option has one chance and every cross or pair option one chance
# (everyday 75 and 65, life events 60 and 50), so each colour's sum is the same in every moment.
# Everyday pairs (one calls two colours, its partner the other three, same stages and ages): the new keeper's rules RG
# (s1) with the choir's auditions WUB (s2); the elders' new order of service WG (s3) with the ward one nurse short UBR
# (s4); the library's notice UG (s1) with the market pitches WBR (s2); the share on the boat UR (s3) with the on-call
# crew WBG (s4); six chairs at the ward forum UR (s1) with the council meeting in the morning WBG (s2); the ward sister
# at eight WR (s3) with the desk officer UBG (s4).
# Life events and their pair sets: the branch splits D1a, the chair of the council D1b, the society splits D2a, the
# players' grant D2b, the free kitchen D3a, the small ward D3b, the evening classes D4a, the makers' association D4b, the
# credit union D5a, the night desk D5b, nominations close D6a, the small claims hearing D6b, the foreman's inquiry D3a.
# Skipped as already written: asked to lead a club or congregation ('the club or congregation asks you to lead'); a
# congregation's leader steps down ('the leader of thirty years steps down'); a club's fee and waiting list ('the club
# adds a waiting list'); rent for a club's room ('the café wants rent for the back room'); a cooperative's roof ('a roof
# three households cannot pay for'); a seat on a residents' body ('a seat on the residents' committee', 'the keys to the
# meeting hall'); the first speech at a council meeting ('the first time you speak at the meeting'); a fight over new
# homes at a meeting ('your town faces a change you could fight'); a fine and its appeal ('a fine you think is unfair');
# a stop and search ('more patrols and more stops'); the street's own watch ('the street starts its own watch'); a strike
# ballot ('a strike vote at work'); a team folding ('the team is down to nine players'); a theatre closing ('the last
# night at the little theatre'); a lean year in a life (left to prod's 'a lean year' in phase 3).

# ================================================================ everyday moments

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls RG (cycle s1); partner: the choir's new director wants auditions

== the new keeper's rules
tier: everyday
sphere: gather
haunt: gather.house
ladder: regular
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-100
calls: RG
rate: .3
share: .2
tone: mixed
life: friends, community, leisure
horizon: months
roles: keeper, regular, friend
who: keeper
earth: your bar has a new keeper, and the notice by the door says no more cards, food at the tables only, and last orders at eleven
times: Among regulars of a bar or café (the third place, Oldenburg 1989), a change of keeper who brings new rules for the room comes to about 1 in 5, once or twice in a life (estimate)
likelier: a regular of some years, a bar or café that has changed hands, a town where rents are rising
rarer: a newcomer, a person who drinks at home, a place run by one family for generations
gap: 5-20
scene: The bar has a new keeper, and the notice by the door says it all: no more cards, food at the tables only, last orders at eleven. The corner where {N} and {friend} play every Thursday is now four tables for diners.
scene W: The new keeper has every right to make rules. But nobody asked the regulars, and some of them have nowhere else to go of an evening.
scene U: {N} wonders what the new keeper is really after: the rent, the diners' money, or a different crowd altogether.
scene B: A bar that changes its crowd changes who meets whom. {N} can already tell which regulars the new keeper wants to keep.
scene R: The cards were the best part of the week. {N} reads the notice twice and feels the heat rise.
scene G: The same faces have sat in that corner for twenty years. Some of them knew the old keeper's father.
- W | ask the new keeper to hear the regulars out on one quiet evening | v: conformity, benevolence | lever: voice | sphere: gather.W | chance: 75
- U | work out what the keeper takes each night, and offer a plan that keeps the cards | v: universalism, achievement | lever: voice | sphere: gather.U | chance: 75
- B | get friendly with the keeper, and have your own table kept as the one exception | act: get friendly with the keeper, and have their own table kept as the one exception | v: power, security | lever: subvert | sphere: gather.B | chance: 75
- R | take the regulars and the cards to the bar across the square that very night | v: stimulation, self-direction | lever: exit | sphere: gather.R | chance: 75
- G | keep coming every Thursday, order the food, and sit where the keeper puts you | act: keep coming every Thursday, order the food, and sit where the keeper puts them | v: tradition, security | lever: loyalty | sphere: gather.G | chance: 75
- W>U | write the regulars' wishes in a short letter, and get every one of them to sign | v: conformity, universalism | lever: voice | sphere: gather.W | chance: 65
- U>B | find out who owns the building, and take the regulars' case over the keeper's head | v: power, self-direction | mark: made an enemy | lever: subvert | sphere: gather.U | chance: 65
- B>R | start a card night at your own kitchen table, with a little in the pot | act: start a card night at their own kitchen table, with a little in the pot | v: hedonism, power | lever: found | sphere: gather.B | chance: 65
- R>G | throw one last loud card night for the old keeper before the rules begin | v: stimulation, tradition | lever: voice | sphere: gather.R | chance: 65
- G>W | come in less, and when you do, sit quietly among the diners | act: come in less, and when they do, sit quietly among the diners | v: security, conformity | lever: neglect | sphere: gather.G | chance: 65
works: The keeper gives the regulars Thursday evenings in the corner, and the cards come back out.
works: The old crowd finds a new home for its nights, and it holds together.
fails: The corner fills with diners, and one by one the old regulars stop coming in.
fails: Words are said at the bar that cannot be taken back, and {N} is not welcome there any more.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WUB (cycle s2); partner: the new keeper's rules

== the choir's new director wants auditions
tier: everyday
sphere: arts
haunt: arts.song
ladder: regular
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-100
calls: WUB
rate: .3
share: .1
tone: mixed
life: music, friends, community
horizon: months
roles: friend, keeper, regular
who: keeper
earth: your community choir has a new director, and from next term everyone must audition again for their place
times: Among people who sing in a community choir or play in a local band (perhaps 1 adult in 10), a new leader who changes who may take part comes to about 1 in 4 of them, once in a life (estimate; overall about 1 in 10 lives)
likelier: a long-standing member of a choir, band or dance group, a group that has grown ambitious, a new leader from outside
rarer: a person who does not sing or play with others, a small group run by friends
gap: 5-20
scene: The community choir has a new director, young and very good, with a plan: a competition in the spring, and from next term everyone sings again for their place. The older altos look at each other. {friend} says she will not do it.
scene W: A community choir is meant to be for anyone who comes. {N} is not sure a choir that tests its own members is still one.
scene U: {N} has heard what the new director can do with sixty voices. The sound could become something real.
scene B: A choir that wins competitions gets heard, booked and talked about. {N} would like a place at the front of that.
scene R: {N} sings because it feels good on a Tuesday night. Nobody ever said anything about tests.
scene G: Some of these singers have stood in the same row for thirty years. The choir sang at their weddings and their parents' funerals.
- W | speak at the choir meeting for a place for anyone who turns up | v: universalism, benevolence | lever: voice | sphere: arts.W | chance: 75
- U | audition, and spend the summer learning your part properly | act: audition, and spend the summer learning their part properly | v: achievement, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | lever: loyalty | sphere: arts.U | chance: 75
- B | try out for the chamber choir in the city, where the best singers go | v: achievement | mark: moved away | lever: exit | sphere: arts.B | chance: 75
- R | start a singing night in the pub's back room, for anyone who wants to sing loud | v: hedonism, stimulation | lever: found | sphere: arts.R | chance: 75
- G | stand with the older altos, and tell the director none of you will audition | act: stand with the older altos, and tell the director none of them will audition | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: voice | sphere: arts.G | chance: 75
- W>B | offer to run the auditions, so every singer gets the same test and a fair hearing | v: conformity, power | lever: loyalty | sphere: arts.W | chance: 65
- U>R | learn the competition piece by ear in a week, and audition with it | v: self-direction, stimulation | lever: loyalty | sphere: arts.U | chance: 65
- B>G | audition, then ask the director to keep the old altos on as a favour to you | act: audition, then ask the director to keep the old altos on as a favour to them | v: power, tradition | lever: subvert | sphere: arts.B | chance: 65
- R>W | tell the director to her face that a choir is for everyone, and see who claps | v: universalism, stimulation | lever: voice | sphere: arts.R | chance: 65
- G>U | keep coming on Tuesdays, but skip the extra rehearsals and the competition | v: tradition, security | lever: neglect | sphere: arts.G | chance: 65
works: The director keeps an open choir beside the competition group, and the old altos stay.
works: {N} finds a place to sing that fits, and the singing is better than it has been in years.
fails: The choir splits down the middle, and the Tuesday nights are never the same again.
fails: {N} loses the place in the row, and with it half the friends of a decade.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WG (cycle s3); partner: the ward one nurse short every night

== the elders' new order of service
tier: everyday
sphere: faith
haunt: faith.congregation
ladder: regular
at: faith body
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-100
calls: WG
rate: .3
share: .15
tone: mixed
life: faith, community, family
horizon: months
roles: elder, parent, friend, regular
who: elder
earth: the elders of your house of worship change the weekly service: newer words, half the length, and the old songs dropped
times: Among people who worship weekly at one house (perhaps 1 adult in 5), elders changing the order of service against the wishes of some of the faithful comes to about 3 in 4 of them over a life (estimate; Chaves 2004 on congregations changing their worship); overall about 1 in 7 lives
likelier: a member of many years, a congregation losing its young, new elders or a new leader
rarer: a person who does not worship, a congregation that never changes its rite
gap: 8-25
scene: The elders have changed the weekly service: newer words, half the length, and songs on a screen instead of the old ones. They say it is to bring the young back. The bench where {Ns} family has sat for three generations is half empty already.
scene W: The elders were chosen to decide such things. Still, nobody asked the congregation, and {N} feels the change ought to have been put to them.
scene U: {N} wants to set the old words and the new ones side by side and see what each one says before deciding anything.
scene B: Every house of worship in town is losing people. The one that finds the young families first will be the one left standing.
scene R: {N} misses the old songs in the chest, not the head. The new ones on the screen feel like music in a shop.
scene G: The old songs were sung at {Ns} grandparents' funerals. To drop them feels like dropping the people who sang them.
- W | write to the elders, and ask that the change be put to the whole congregation | v: conformity, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: group congregation | sphere: faith.W | chance: 75
- U | sit with the new words for a whole season before you judge them | act: sit with the new words for a whole season before they judge them | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: loyalty | sphere: faith.U | chance: 75
- B | move to the house of worship across town that is still full | v: security, achievement | lever: exit | sphere: faith.B | chance: 75
- R | start singing the old songs with friends in a kitchen on rest day evenings | v: hedonism, tradition | lever: found | sphere: faith.R | chance: 75
- G | come only on the holy days, when the old songs are still sung | v: tradition | lever: neglect | sphere: faith.G | chance: 75
- W>R | stand up at the congregation's meeting and ask why nobody was asked | v: universalism, self-direction | mark: defied an authority | lever: voice | pushes: group congregation | sphere: faith.W | chance: 65
- U>G | find the old song books, copy them, and give one to every family who asks | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | sphere: faith.U | chance: 65
- B>W | quietly offer the elder who chose the songs your help, for one old song | act: quietly offer the elder who chose the songs their help, for one old song | v: power, conformity | lever: subvert | sphere: faith.B | chance: 65
- R>U | ask the young ones what they make of the new service, and tell the elders straight | v: self-direction, benevolence | lever: voice | sphere: faith.R | chance: 65
- G>B | give your tithe to the old house in your family's village instead | act: give their tithe to the old house in their family's village instead | v: tradition, security | lever: exit | sphere: faith.G | chance: 65
works: The elders agree to one old song every week, and the half-empty bench fills again.
works: {N} finds a way to keep the old songs alive that the whole family can share.
fails: The congregation splits into those who stay and those who go, and {Ns} family is on both sides.
fails: Nothing changes, and {N} sits through the new service every week feeling like a guest.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UBR (cycle s4); partner: the elders' new order of service

== the ward one nurse short every night
tier: everyday
sphere: care
haunt: care.houses
ladder: regular
at: hospital
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-100
calls: UBR
rate: .3
share: .1
tone: trouble
life: work, health, money
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, friend
who: colleague
earth: on your hospital ward the night shift is down to one nurse and you, the pay is frozen again, and the agency down the road pays a third more
times: Among nurses, care assistants and ward volunteers (perhaps 1 adult in 15 works or helps on a ward at some time), a lean year of short shifts and frozen pay comes to most of them at least once (estimate; short staffing and its costs, Aiken et al. 2002); overall about 1 in 10 lives
likelier: a care assistant, nurse or porter, a public hospital in a lean budget year, a ward far from the city
rarer: a person who never works in care, a well-funded hospital, a year of new hiring
gap: 3-12
scene: The night shift on {Ns} ward is down to one nurse and {N}, for twenty-eight beds. The pay is frozen for the second year, and the agency down the road pays a third more for the same hours. {colleague} hands in her notice on Monday.
scene W: Every patient on the ward deserves the same care at three in the morning as at noon. With two pairs of hands, that is no longer true.
scene U: {N} keeps a note of the falls, the late medicines and the call bells left ringing. The figures tell a plain story.
scene B: The agency pays more and lets its nurses choose their shifts. Staying out of loyalty is a choice {N} can no longer quite afford.
scene R: When the call bell goes, {N} runs. Night after night of it, and the running is wearing thin.
scene G: Some of the old patients have been on this ward before, and they ask for {N} by name.
- W | ask the union to put the night rota before the hospital board | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | pushes: institution hospital | sphere: care.W | chance: 75
- U | write down every fall and late medicine for a month, and send the list up | v: universalism, achievement | lever: voice | pushes: institution hospital | sphere: care.U | chance: 75
- B | sign with the agency, and come back to the same ward at agency rates | v: achievement, power | lever: exit | sphere: care.B | chance: 75
- R | take the extra night shifts while they last, and run between the bells | v: stimulation, benevolence | lever: loyalty | sphere: care.R | chance: 75
- G | stay on for the patients who ask for you by name | act: stay on for the patients who ask for them by name | v: benevolence, tradition | lever: loyalty | sphere: care.G | chance: 75
- W>G | start a carers' circle so the night staff can cover each other's sick days | v: benevolence, universalism | lever: found | sphere: care.W | chance: 65
- U>W | work out a fairer rota from the figures, and pin it up for the whole ward | v: universalism, achievement | lever: voice | sphere: care.U | chance: 65
- B>U | do your hours and no more, and keep your strength for the agency shifts | act: do their hours and no more, and keep their strength for the agency shifts | v: security, achievement | lever: neglect | sphere: care.B | chance: 65
- R>B | swap shifts with the agency nurse off the books, for cash in hand | v: stimulation, power | mark: broke the law | lever: subvert | sphere: care.R | chance: 65
- G>R | leave the ward with the rest of the night staff, all on the same day | v: benevolence, self-direction | lever: exit | pushes: institution hospital | sphere: care.G | chance: 65
works: The board takes on two night nurses, and the bells are answered again.
works: {N} finds a way through the lean year that keeps both the patients and {Ns} own health.
fails: {N} is worn to the bone by spring, and the ward is no better staffed for it.
fails: The ward closes six beds, and the patients who would have filled them go elsewhere or nowhere.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UG (cycle s1); partner: the warden redraws the market pitches

== the notice on the library desk
tier: everyday
sphere: learn
haunt: learn.keeping
ladder: newcomer
at: council
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-100
calls: UG
rate: .3
share: .15
tone: mixed
life: learning, community, neighbours
horizon: months
roles: neighbour, friend, keeper
who: keeper
earth: you have only just joined the branch library, and a notice says it will close in the spring unless volunteers run it
times: Many towns have cut library hours or handed branches to volunteers in lean budget years (estimate); being a new user of a branch when it faces closure comes to about 1 in 7 lives, once (estimate)
likelier: a person new to town, a parent of young children, a person out of work, a town in a lean budget year
rarer: a person who never uses a library, a city with a well-funded library service
gap: 10-30
scene: {N} only joined the branch library this winter, and already knows the librarian's name. Now there is a notice on the desk: the council is cutting its hours, and unless volunteers run it from the spring, the branch closes for good.
scene W: A library lends to anyone who walks in, whatever they can pay. {N} has needed that more than once this year.
scene U: For {N} the library is the one quiet room in town with all the answers in it.
scene B: If volunteers run it, they will decide what it becomes. Someone new could have a say from the very start.
scene R: {N} has been coming for two months. It seems unfair to lose it just as it had become a second home.
scene G: The old regulars say their grandparents learned to read in this room.
- W | put your name down for a Saturday on the volunteers' desk | act: put their name down for a Saturday on the volunteers' desk | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: helped someone in need | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution council | sphere: learn.W | chance: 75
- U | read the council's figures, and show the meeting what the branch really costs | v: universalism, achievement | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: learn.U | chance: 75
- B | join the big library in the city, and borrow there on the way to work | v: security, achievement | lever: exit | sphere: learn.B | chance: 75
- R | sign the petition on the desk, and bring ten more names by Friday | v: stimulation, universalism | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: learn.R | chance: 75
- G | leave the fight to the old regulars, and keep your head down as the new one | act: leave the fight to the old regulars, and keep their head down as the new one | v: conformity, security | lever: neglect | sphere: learn.G | chance: 75
- W>U | help the librarian write down what the volunteers will need to know before she goes | v: benevolence, universalism | lever: loyalty | sphere: learn.W | chance: 65
- U>B | borrow the old local records on a friend's card, so they are not sent away | v: self-direction, power | lever: subvert | sphere: learn.U | chance: 65
- B>R | get the café next door to take a shelf of the books, and run it yourself | act: get the café next door to take a shelf of the books, and run it themselves | v: achievement, stimulation | lever: found | sphere: learn.B | chance: 65
- R>G | knock on doors along your street, and ask who learned to read in that room | act: knock on doors along their street, and ask who learned to read in that room | v: stimulation, tradition | lever: voice | sphere: learn.R | chance: 65
- G>W | start a reading circle in your front room for the old regulars, should the branch go | act: start a reading circle in their front room for the old regulars, should the branch go | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: found | sphere: learn.G | chance: 65
works: Enough volunteers come forward, and the branch stays open on four days a week.
works: {N} finds friends among the regulars, and a place in the town that did not exist a season ago.
fails: The branch closes in the spring, and the building is boarded up by the summer.
fails: The volunteers quarrel over who runs what, and {N}, the newest of them, takes the blame.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WBR (cycle s2); partner: the notice on the library desk

== the warden redraws the market pitches
tier: everyday
sphere: comm
haunt: comm.market
ladder: regular
at: council
stages: young_adult+
age: 18-100
calls: WBR
rate: .3
share: .1
tone: trouble
life: work, money, family
horizon: months
roles: parent, sibling, rival, keeper
who: keeper
earth: the market warden redraws the pitches by lot, and your family's corner pitch of twenty years goes to a stranger with a van
times: Among people who work a market stall (perhaps 1 adult in 15 at some time), a warden redrawing or repricing the pitches comes to about 1 in 3 of them (estimate); overall about 1 in 20 lives
likelier: a family stall of long standing, a market run by the council, a market with a waiting list for pitches
rarer: a person who never works a stall, a market owned by its own traders
gap: 5-20
scene: The market warden has redrawn the pitches by lot, to be fair to the newcomers, he says. The corner pitch {Ns} family has worked for twenty years goes to a stranger with a van, and {N} is moved to the windy end by the bins.
scene W: A draw is fair on paper. But twenty years of turning up at five in the morning ought to count for something.
scene U: {N} works out what the corner was worth in a week's takings, and what the windy end will be worth.
scene B: The warden has friends. {N} knows two stallholders who kept their pitches and were never in the draw.
scene R: {N} wants to walk up to the van, stand on the corner and dare the stranger to unload.
scene G: The regulars have bought their bread from that corner since {N} was a child. They will look for it there.
- W | accept the draw, and wish the newcomer well on the corner | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | sphere: comm.W | chance: 75
- U | write to the council with the takings, and ask for the draw to be done again | v: universalism, achievement | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: comm.U | chance: 75
- B | find out which of the warden's friends kept their pitches, and use it quietly | v: power | lever: subvert | sphere: comm.B | chance: 75
- R | pack up and take the stall to the Saturday market in the next town | v: stimulation, self-direction | lever: exit | sphere: comm.R | chance: 75
- G | keep the family stall at the windy end, and send word to every regular | v: tradition, security | lever: loyalty | sphere: comm.G | chance: 75
- W>B | ask the council for a rule that counts years at the market in the next draw | v: conformity, power | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: comm.W | chance: 65
- U>R | turn the windy end into a hot food stall for the cold mornings | v: achievement, stimulation | lever: found | sphere: comm.U | chance: 65
- B>G | offer the stranger a fair price for the corner pitch, off the books | v: power, tradition | lever: subvert | sphere: comm.B | chance: 65
- R>W | tell the warden at the traders' meeting, in front of everyone, that the draw was rigged | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: voice | sphere: comm.R | chance: 65
- G>U | leave the stall to a cousin on market days, and look for steadier work | v: security | lever: neglect | sphere: comm.G | chance: 65
works: The regulars follow the stall to its new place, and the takings hold.
works: The next draw counts years at the market, and the family gets its corner back.
fails: The windy end is half the trade, and by winter the family is talking about giving the stall up.
fails: The quarrel with the warden goes on for a year, and {N} is on the wrong end of every ruling.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UR (cycle s3); partner: the on-call crew is two short

== the new owner ends the share
tier: everyday
sphere: prod
haunt: prod.wild
ladder: regular
at: employer
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 18-64
calls: UR
rate: .3
share: .05
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, parent
who: boss
earth: the boat's new owner ends the old share of the catch and puts the crew on a day wage, take it or leave it
times: Fishing crews in many places are still paid by a share of the catch (the lay); among crew, an owner moving them to wages comes to about 1 in 3 over a working life (estimate; Acheson 1981 on fishing crews), and like the share changing on any crew paid by the result, it reaches about 1 in 20 lives
likelier: a fishing, farm or harvest crew paid by shares, a boat sold out of the family, a run of lean seasons
rarer: a wage earner in a firm, a crew of cousins who own their boat
gap: 5-20
scene: The boat has a new owner, and on the quay he tells the crew: no more shares of the catch, a day wage from now on, take it or leave it. A good season's share paid twice that. {colleague} has already walked off down the harbour.
scene W: The old share was the same rule for everyone aboard, skipper included. A wage makes the crew hired hands.
scene U: {N} works it through: a wage is safe in a lean year, the share wins in a good one. Which kind of year is coming?
scene B: A wage is a wage. And a hand who backs the new owner now may be the mate by next season.
scene R: {N} did not go to sea to be paid by the hour. The share was the gamble, and the gamble was the point.
scene G: {Ns} father fished on the share, and his father before him. It is how the harbour has always worked.
- W | ask the owner to set the wage beside the share in front of the crew | v: universalism, conformity | lever: voice | sphere: prod.W | chance: 75
- U | take the wage for one season, and note what the share would have paid | v: security, achievement | lever: loyalty | sphere: prod.U | chance: 75
- B | work only for the wage, and save your effort for a boat of your own | act: work only for the wage, and save their effort for a boat of their own | v: achievement, security | lever: neglect | sphere: prod.B | chance: 75
- R | walk off with the others, and sign on with a boat that still pays shares | v: self-direction, stimulation | lever: exit | sphere: prod.R | chance: 75
- G | stay with the crew of cousins, whatever they decide on the quay | v: tradition, conformity | lever: loyalty | sphere: prod.G | chance: 75
- W>R | call every crew in the harbour to the quay to talk about the new wages | v: universalism, stimulation | lever: voice | pushes: group work | sphere: prod.W | chance: 65
- U>G | club together with three of the crew for a small boat on the old share | v: self-direction, tradition | mark: took a wild risk | lever: found | sphere: prod.U | chance: 65
