# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: the society splits (pairs D2a)

== the society splits over the open nights
tier: life event
sphere: gather
haunt: gather.hall
ladder: leader
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .02 .02 .02
drivers: community+.2 money-.2
times: Among people who chair a club, lodge or society (perhaps 1 adult in 30 at some time), a split between old members and new ones over who may come comes to about 1 in 3 over their years in the chair (estimate; Putnam 2000 on clubs that age with their members), set at 2 in 100 a year among them
likelier: a society whose members are ageing, a town with many newcomers, a hall that needs costly repairs
rarer: a young society, a club with a long waiting list, a hall owned outright with money in the bank
gap: 6-20
tone: mixed
life: community, friends, money
horizon: years
roles: friend, rival, elder, regular
who: regular
earth: the society you chair is splitting: the old members want it kept as it was, the young ones want open nights, and the roof needs paying for
scene: {N} has chaired the society for six years. Now it is splitting: the old members want the hall kept for members as it always was, the young ones want open nights for anyone, and the roof needs mending before winter. The annual meeting is in a fortnight.
scene W: A society is its members, all of them. {N} wants whatever happens to be decided by them, in the open.
scene U: {N} has the figures: members falling every year, a roof that costs more than a year's dues. The numbers say something must change.
scene B: Whoever wins the annual meeting will run the hall for a decade. {N} would like that to still be {N}.
scene R: The young ones' open night last month was the best evening the hall has had in years. {N} wants more of it.
scene G: The old members' grandparents built this society. Their names are on the boards in the hall.
- W | put both plans to the annual meeting, and abide by the members' vote | v: conformity, universalism | lever: lead | pushes: group club | sphere: gather.W | chance: 60
- U | run the open nights for one season as a trial, and show the meeting the figures | v: achievement, universalism | lever: lead | sphere: gather.U | chance: 60
- B | pay for the roof yourself, on the understanding that the open nights go ahead | act: pay for the roof themselves, on the understanding that the open nights go ahead | v: power, achievement | lever: fund | sphere: gather.B | chance: 60
- R | throw the next open night before the vote, and let the hall decide for itself | v: stimulation, hedonism | lever: lead | sphere: gather.R | chance: 60
- G | stand by the old members who built the society, and keep the hall as it was | v: tradition, security | mark: kept your word | lever: loyalty | pushes: group club | sphere: gather.G | chance: 60
- WU | write a new rule book with a members' night and an open night each week | v: universalism, conformity | lever: lead | sphere: gather.WU | chance: 50
- WG | hold a supper for both sides at the hall, and ask them to stay one society | v: benevolence, tradition | lever: loyalty | sphere: gather.GW | chance: 50
- UR | step down as chair, and help the young ones start a society of their own | v: self-direction, benevolence | lever: found | sphere: gather.UR | chance: 50
- BR | get the hall licensed for late open nights, through a friend on the licensing board | v: power, hedonism | lever: office | sphere: gather.BR | chance: 50
- BG | let the treasurer quietly bury the young ones' plan in the accounts | v: power, tradition | mark: hid a wrong | lever: subvert | sphere: gather.BG | chance: 50
works: The society holds together with two kinds of night, and the roof is mended by the first frost.
works: The hall fills again, and {N} hands on a living society instead of a dying one.
fails: Half the members walk out, and the society that is left cannot pay for the roof.
fails: The annual meeting turns bitter, and {N} loses the chair to the side {N} did not choose.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: the players' grant is cut (pairs D2b)

== the players' grant is cut
tier: life event
sphere: arts
haunt: arts.stage
ladder: pillar
at: council
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .015 .015 .01
drivers: money-.3 harsh+.2
times: Among people who direct or run an amateur or small company (perhaps 1 adult in 100), a council grant cut that threatens the season comes to about 1 in 3 over their years at the head of it (estimate; such grants are among the first cut in lean budget years), set at 1 to 2 in 100 a year among them
likelier: a company in a council-owned theatre, a lean budget year, a town with few other patrons
rarer: a company that pays its own way, a city with many funders
gap: 6-20
tone: trouble
life: interests, community, money
horizon: years
roles: friend, mentor, colleague, patron
who: patron
earth: the council cuts the grant that keeps the community players in their theatre, and the company you have directed for years looks to you
scene: {N} has directed the community players in the old theatre on the square for fifteen years. This week the council cut the grant that pays for the lights and the heating. Without it the spring season is gone, and the company's young ones are already talking about the city.
scene W: The players have always been for the whole town: a place on stage for anyone who turns up to the reading. {N} wants that to survive.
scene U: {N} knows which plays fill the seats and which ones empty them. A season could be built to pay, if it had to be.
scene B: Fifteen years have made {Ns} name in this town. A name like that can open doors, or a purse.
scene R: {N} is furious. The council spends more on its own dinners, and someone ought to say so on a stage.
scene G: The players were founded by the old families of the square, and their children still paint the sets.
- W | hold the company together, unpaid, and stage the spring season in the school hall | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | lever: loyalty | sphere: arts.W | chance: 60
- U | rebuild the season around the plays that sell, and show the council the takings | v: achievement, universalism | lever: lead | sphere: arts.U | chance: 60
- B | take your best players to the big company in the city, under your own name | act: take their best players to the big company in the city, under their own name | v: achievement, power | mark: moved away | lever: exit | sphere: arts.B | chance: 60
- R | stage a satire of the council's cuts on the theatre steps, and invite the press | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: defied an authority | lever: voice | sphere: arts.R | chance: 60
- G | keep teaching the old plays to the young ones, whatever stage you end up on | act: keep teaching the old plays to the young ones, whatever stage they end up on | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | sphere: arts.G | chance: 60
- WB | take a seat on the town's arts board, and argue for the theatre's grant there | v: power, universalism | lever: office | pushes: institution council | sphere: arts.WB | chance: 50
- WR | lead the whole company, in costume, to the council's next meeting | v: universalism, stimulation | lever: voice | pushes: institution council | sphere: arts.RW | chance: 50
- UB | start a theatre school in the old hall, and let its fees pay for the stage | v: achievement, self-direction | lever: found | sphere: arts.UB | chance: 50
- UG | pay for one young player's place each year, in the founders' name | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: fund | sphere: arts.GU | chance: 50
- RG | make the young ones the company's board, and stage whatever they choose | v: self-direction, tradition | lever: lead | sphere: arts.RG | chance: 50
works: The spring season goes ahead, and the town turns out for it as it never did when it was paid for.
works: The players find a way to stand on their own, and {Ns} name is on the new posters.
fails: The theatre goes dark for the spring, and some of the players never come back.
fails: {N} fights the council for a year, and wins back half a grant and a lasting enemy in the chamber.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: the free kitchen is running out (pairs D3a)

== the kitchen in the back hall is running out
tier: life event
sphere: faith
haunt: faith.congregation
ladder: leader
at: faith body
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .02 .02 .02
drivers: money-.3 harsh+.3
times: Most congregations run some work of mercy, a meal, a food store or a shelter (Chaves 2004); among those who lead a congregation (perhaps 1 adult in 200), its free kitchen or food store running out of money comes to about 1 in 3 over their years as leader (estimate), set at 2 in 100 a year among them
likelier: a congregation in a poor district, a lean year, an ageing band of volunteers
rarer: a rich congregation, a town where the council feeds the hungry
gap: 6-20
tone: trouble
life: faith, community, money
horizon: years
roles: elder, friend, neighbour, patron
who: patron
earth: the free kitchen your congregation runs in its back hall has three months of money left, and as its leader the choice is yours
scene: For twenty years the congregation has fed anyone who comes to the back hall on Fridays. Now the money will last three months, the volunteers are old, and the queue is longer than ever. As the congregation's leader, {N} must say at the next gathering what happens.
scene W: Feeding whoever comes is the plainest duty there is. {N} cannot stand up at the gathering and say it ends.
scene U: {N} wants to know who comes now, and why there are more of them, before deciding what the kitchen should be.
scene B: A kitchen with a name over the door draws gifts. A kitchen run on goodwill alone is running out.
scene R: {N} thinks of the faces in the queue last Friday, and the answer seems obvious: more nights, not fewer.
scene G: The kitchen was started by the old families' mothers, who cooked what their own mothers cooked.
- W | tell the congregation plainly what it costs, and ask every household for a share | v: benevolence, conformity | lever: lead | pushes: group congregation | sphere: faith.W | chance: 60
- U | join the kitchen to the town's food bank, so it runs on their stores | v: universalism, security | lever: lead | sphere: faith.U | chance: 60
- B | give a large gift of your own, and name the kitchen for your family | act: give a large gift of their own, and name the kitchen for their family | v: power, benevolence | lever: fund | sphere: faith.B | chance: 60
- R | preach on it at the gathering, so that nobody leaves without giving | v: stimulation, benevolence | lever: voice | pushes: group congregation | sphere: faith.R | chance: 60
- G | ask the old families who started it to come back and cook as their mothers did | v: tradition, benevolence | lever: loyalty | sphere: faith.G | chance: 60
- WU | stand for the council in May, with the kitchen's grant as your one promise | act: stand for the council in May, with the kitchen's grant as their one promise | v: universalism, power | lever: office | pushes: institution council | sphere: faith.WU | chance: 50
- WR | open the kitchen on more nights, and trust the help to come | v: benevolence, stimulation | mark: helped someone in need | lever: lead | sphere: faith.RW | chance: 50
- UB | set up a trust for the kitchen, with its own board and its own books | v: achievement, security | lever: found | sphere: faith.UB | chance: 50
- RG | hand the kitchen to the young ones, and let them run it their own way | v: self-direction, tradition | lever: lead | sphere: faith.RG | chance: 50
- BG | ask the councillor who worships with you for a ward grant, as a favour | act: ask the councillor who worships with them for a ward grant, as a favour | v: power, tradition | lever: subvert | sphere: faith.BG | chance: 50
works: The kitchen stays open on Fridays, and the queue is fed as it always was.
works: The kitchen finds a new footing that will outlast {N}, and the congregation is proud of it.
fails: The kitchen closes in the summer, and {N} hears the queue moved to the station steps.
fails: The money comes, but the quarrel over who runs the kitchen splits the gathering.

