who: rival
earth: the old warden of the makers' association retires, and the masters want you to judge the apprentices' final pieces and keep the standard
scene: The old warden of the makers' association has retired after twenty years of judging apprentices' final pieces. The masters want {N} to take it on. The trouble is known to all: half the association passes its own nephews, and the other half wants the test scrapped.
scene W: A test is only worth something if it is the same for everyone. {N} has seen good apprentices fail and nephews pass.
scene U: Nobody has ever written down what makes a piece pass. It lives in the old warden's head, and he has gone.
scene B: The warden decides who gets to call themselves a master. That is a great deal of power in a small trade.
scene R: {N} would rather be at the bench making things than in a back room judging other people's.
scene G: The old warden taught {N}, and his teacher taught him. The standard is a thing passed hand to hand.
