# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: lost fairly at the small claims hearing (pairs D6b)

== lost fairly at the small claims hearing
tier: life event
sphere: rule
at: court
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
per_year: 0 0 .004 .006 .005 .003
drivers: money+.2 trouble+.2
times: Bringing or defending a small claim is uncommon but not rare (about 1 adult in 15 over a life; estimate); losing one after a hearing that was plainly fair comes to about 1 in 30 lives (estimate). Tyler 1990 and Tyler and Huo 2002: people who lose after a fair hearing (heard, treated with respect, given reasons) accept the ruling and keep faith in the court far more than those who win or lose after an unfair one
likelier: a homeowner, a person who hires builders or sells goods, a person who has been let down by a trader
rarer: a person who never signs contracts, a person who settles every quarrel out of court
gap: 5-20
tone: mixed
life: law, money, home
horizon: months
roles: partner, friend, parent
who: partner
earth: you lose your claim against the builder at the small claims hearing, though the clerk and the judge heard every word you had to say
scene: {N} sued the builder who left the kitchen half done. The clerk explained every step, and the judge let {N} speak for as long as it took and read every photograph. Then she ruled for the builder: the contract {N} signed had allowed the delay.
scene W: It was done properly: both sides heard, the reasons given. {N} lost fairly, and that is a strange thing to feel.
scene U: {N} goes over the judge's reasons at home, and has to admit the contract says what she said it says.
scene B: The builder's man knew the forms better. Next time {N} will read what is signed, and bring someone who knows.
scene R: {N} is still angry at the builder, and the judge's kind manner almost makes it worse.
scene G: {Ns} parent always said courts were for people with money. Today that was not quite true, and {N} is not sure what to make of it.
- W | accept the ruling, pay the costs, and thank the clerk on the way out | v: conformity, universalism | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.W | chance: 60
- U | read the judgment through at home, and accept it once you see the reasons | act: read the judgment through at home, and accept it once they see the reasons | v: universalism, self-direction | mark: learned a skill | lever: loyalty | sphere: rule.U | chance: 60
- B | find a lawyer who can find an opening, and appeal on a point of law | v: power, achievement | lever: voice | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.B | chance: 60
- R | write a hard review of the builder online, ruling or no ruling | v: self-direction, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | lever: voice | tech: internet | sphere: rule.R | chance: 60
- G | settle it the old way, and ask the builder's family to make it right | v: tradition, security | lever: exit | sphere: rule.G | chance: 60
- WU | write to the advice office, so other buyers know about that kind of contract | v: universalism, benevolence | lever: voice | sphere: rule.WU | chance: 50
- WG | pay up, and tell the street that the court was fair, whatever they say | v: conformity, tradition | mark: owned up | lever: loyalty | pushes: institution court | sphere: rule.GW | chance: 50
- UB | ask the clerk how an appeal works, and file the form on the last day allowed | v: achievement, self-direction | lever: voice | sphere: rule.UB | chance: 50
- BR | hold back the last payment you still owe the builder, ruling or no ruling | act: hold back the last payment they still owe the builder, ruling or no ruling | v: power, self-direction | mark: broke the law | lever: subvert | sphere: rule.BR | chance: 50
- RG | leave the costs letter unopened on the side for a month, and hope it goes away | v: hedonism, security | lever: neglect | sphere: rule.RG | chance: 50
works: {N} pays, reads every contract twice from then on, and finds the court a place {N} would trust again.
works: The builder, shamed by the hearing more than the ruling, comes back and finishes the kitchen.
fails: {N} fights on, loses again, and the costs double.
fails: The loss sours {N} on the law for years, fair hearing or not.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: the foreman's inquiry (pairs D3a)

== the foreman's inquiry into the jammed press
tier: life event
sphere: prod
at: employer
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 18-67
per_year: 0 0 .004 .004 .003 0
drivers: harsh+.2 money-.1
times: Among people who work with machines (perhaps 1 adult in 4 at some time), an accident on the shift and an inquiry that finds part of the fault with them comes to about 1 in 15 (estimate); overall about 1 in 60 lives. Tyler 1990: when a boss hears everyone and gives reasons, a ruling against a worker is far more often accepted, and the rule kept afterwards
likelier: a machine operator or line hand, a works with old machines, a shift where checks are often skipped
rarer: an office worker, a works with a strong safety record
gap: 5-20
tone: trouble
life: work, health
horizon: months
roles: colleague, boss, parent, friend
who: boss
earth: after the press jams and a workmate's hand is cut, the foreman's inquiry hears everyone fairly and finds that you skipped the guard check; a warning goes on your file
scene: When the press jammed and {colleague} cut a hand, the foreman held an inquiry. He heard everyone, {N} included, as long as they needed, and read the logbook aloud. His finding: {N} skipped the guard check that morning. A written warning goes on {Ns} file.
scene W: It was a fair hearing, and the log does not lie. {N} skipped the check because everyone did, and that is no excuse.
scene U: {N} goes back over the morning. The check was skipped, but the guard had been loose for weeks, and the log says that too.
scene B: A warning on file means no foreman's post for three years. {N} wonders who else could share the blame.
scene R: {N} feels sick about the hand, and furious at being the only name in the report.
scene G: {Ns} father worked that floor for thirty years and never had a mark against him.
